 "api_error_status": null,
 "result": "They're all **context-local proxies** built on a `ContextVar`, using werkzeug's `LocalProxy`.\n\n**`src/tessel/globals.py`**\n- `_app_var: ContextVar[AppContext]` (line 40) holds the current `AppContext`.\n- `active_app`, `g`, `request`, `session` and `app_ctx` are each a `werkzeug.local.LocalProxy` over `_app_var`. Each one resolves a different attribute of the current `AppContext`:\n  - `app_ctx` is the context itself.\n  - `active_app` is `.app`, the `Tessel` instance.\n  - `g` is `.g`, an `_AppCtxGlobals`.\n  - `request` is `.request`.\n  - `session` is `.session`.\n- If no context is set, they raise a \"Working outside of application/request context\" error (`unbound_message`). That is why code such as `if not active_app:` works as an \"is there a context?\" check.\n\n**`src/tessel/ctx.py`**\n- `AppContext` is the object that holds `app`, `g`, `request` and `session`.\n- Pushing it calls `_app_var.set(self)` (line 433) and stores the token.\n- Popping it calls `_app_var.reset(self._cv_token)` (line 498).\n- `app.app_context()` and the request-handling paths in `src/tessel/app.py` do the push and pop.\n\nBecause `ContextVar` is per-thread and per-async-task, each thread or task sees its own context.\n\n`request_ctx` is a deprecated alias for `app_ctx`, because the request context has been merged into the app context.",
